<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Golan Heights</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Golan Heights is a basalt plateau in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hermon">Mount Hermon</a>. In antiquity it held the Aramaean kingdoms of Geshur and Maacah, and later Ghassanid camps such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabiyah">Jabiyah</a>. Part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> after 1946, most of it was occupied by Israel in 1967 and annexed in 1981, a step not recognised by most of the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golan-heights.jpg" alt="Golan Heights" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Goran_tek-en · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golan_Heights_Map-SVG.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Golan is a basaltic plateau at the south-western corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, generally bounded by the Jordan Valley on the west, the Yarmouk River on the south and the Sa'ar stream on the north, with its eastern limit at the Ruqqad or the Allan river. It is about 65 km long and 12 to 25 km wide and overlooks the Jordan Rift Valley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a>. Volcanic eruptions that began about 4 million years ago formed it, and dormant volcanoes and cinder cones are scattered across it. The snow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hermon">Mount Hermon</a>, 2,814 m high, feeds springs and rivers in the Jordan watershed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest evidence of habitation dates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-paleolithic">Upper Paleolithic</a>, and settlement grew from the 2nd millennium BCE. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Late Bronze Age collapse</a> the kingdom of Geshur emerged; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> describes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>'s marriage to Maacha, daughter of Talmai, king of Geshur, as a dynastic alliance. By the mid-9th century BCE Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> had absorbed Geshur, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> conquered the region in the 8th century BCE. Gaulanitis, the Greek name for the region, appears after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s conquest, under Ptolemaic and then Seleucid rule. In the middle of the 2nd century BCE the Itureans moved in, and the Roman and Byzantine periods left Diocletianic boundary stones and 25 ancient synagogues.</p><p>After the Battle of Yarmouk the Golan came under Muawiyah I, the future first Umayyad caliph, and then passed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>. Crusader forces held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banias">Banias</a> in 1128–32 and 1140–64, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> later ruled the area. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> swept through in 1259 and were driven off by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutuz">Qutuz</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a> in 1260. In the 1560s the Ottoman official Mustafa Lala Pasha made al-Qunaytira a regional centre.</p><p>When the French mandate ended in 1946 the Golan became part of the independent Syrian Arab Republic. Israeli forces captured the plateau on 9–10 June 1967 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a>, and an estimated 80,000 to 130,000 people fled or were driven out. After the 1973 war a third of the plateau returned to Syrian administration. In 1981 the Knesset passed the Golan Heights Law applying Israeli law there, which the UN Security Council condemned in Resolution 497.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Golan Heights” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army lost two-thirds of the <b>Golan Heights</b> to Israel in under 48 hours in the Six-Day War of 1967, retreated from Jordan in Black September 1970, and with Egypt launched the Yom Kippur War on 6 October 1973, in which Israel reversed its gains and destroyed Quneitra.</blockquote><a href="/empire/syrian-arab-republic">Syrian Arab Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Fighting ended with Israel holding Sinai, Gaza, the West Bank, East Jerusalem and the <b>Golan Heights</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-10">10 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In six days Israel took Sinai, Gaza, the West Bank, East Jerusalem and the <b>Golan Heights</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-5">5 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Egyptian and Syrian forces launched surprise attacks on Israeli positions in Sinai and the <b>Golan Heights</b> on the holiest day of the Jewish year.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-6">6 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Maacah lay in the northern <b>Golan</b> and the slopes of Mount Hermon, north of Geshur.</blockquote><a href="/state/aram-maacah">Aram-Maacah</a></li><li><blockquote>Geshur occupied the southern <b>Golan</b> and the north-eastern shore of the Sea of Galilee.</blockquote><a href="/state/geshur">Geshur</a></li></ul></section>
